# Practical editorial resources: complete local reading pages, with no placeholder downloads.
GUIDES=[
 ('find-the-friction','Operations','Where Is the Friction in Your Business?','Look beyond the symptom and follow one piece of work from start to finish.','resources-kitchen','Construction workers coordinating activity at a building site',[
 ('Start With One Real Example','Choose something recent: a customer who waited, an estimate that stalled, or a task that returned to you three times. Describe what actually happened, rather than what the process was supposed to be. A specific example gives the discussion a useful starting point.','Write down where the work began, which people touched it, what information they needed, and how it reached the next step. Include the waiting and checking that happened between the obvious tasks.'),
 ('Look at People, Systems, and Process','For people, ask whether someone clearly owned the work and had the capacity and information to complete it. For systems, ask where the information lived and whether the next person could find it. For process, ask whether the next action and the definition of complete were clear.','A missed call can involve all three. The team may be occupied, the message may sit in an isolated inbox, and nobody may own returning it. Buying another tool before understanding that path could leave the original gap in place.'),
 ('Find the Point That Creates Repeated Work','Notice where someone asks for the same information, copies it between tools, waits for an answer, or sends the work back. Ask what caused that extra step. Sometimes an approval is necessary. Sometimes the approval exists because ownership or decision boundaries were never clear.','Choose one point to examine first. Consider customer impact, frequency, effort, and how much depends on the owner. You do not need to redesign the whole business to learn something useful.'),
 ('Try One Clear Change','Agree who owns the next step, where the information belongs, and how you will know the work is complete. Explain the change to the people who use it. Review several real examples after it has been used.','Compare what happened with your starting example. Did the waiting decrease? Did the same questions return? Did a different problem appear? Keep what helps, adjust what does not, and use the evidence to choose the next step.')],
 'Pick one recurring task. Record its trigger, owner, information, next step, and completion point. Circle the handoff that produces the most waiting or repeated work.'),
 ('customer-inquiry','Customers','Follow One Customer Inquiry.','A practical way to see where calls, messages, and next steps can fall through.','resources-barber','A barber speaking with a customer during a service',[
 ('Begin Where the Customer Begins','Choose a recent inquiry and follow its actual route. Did it arrive by phone, email, a website form, or a social message? Who saw it first? What happened while the person responsible was serving another customer?','Look across all the places inquiries arrive. A process that works well for calls can still leave email or website messages waiting. The customer experiences your business as one relationship, even when the messages enter through different tools.'),
 ('Give the Next Step an Owner','Acknowledging a message and completing the customer’s request are different actions. Record who is responsible for each one. If a message needs an estimate, appointment, or technical answer, make the handoff explicit.','Agree what information the next person needs, where the request is recorded, and what happens if the usual owner is unavailable. Avoid relying on a forwarded message as the only evidence that someone has accepted responsibility.'),
 ('Make Follow-Up Visible','Use a place your team can actually maintain to record the inquiry, its current owner, and the next action. The right starting point depends on the business; a more complicated system is not automatically more useful.','Look for requests that are open without a next action, messages that have changed owners several times, and customers who had to ask twice. Those examples can reveal a missing handoff or an unclear completion point.'),
 ('Review the Experience, Not Just the Count','A high number of answered messages does not tell you whether customers got a useful response. Read or discuss a small set of real examples with the team. Consider how long customers waited and whether their next step was clear.','Choose one improvement, then review the same part of the journey again. Response expectations should reflect your hours, capacity, customer needs, and the work involved.')],
 'Take one recent inquiry. Can you identify its owner, its next action, and what the customer was told to expect?'),
 ('delegate-with-clarity','Time','Before You Delegate, Make the Work Clear.','Separate the decisions that need your judgment from the recurring work that needs ownership.','owner-laptop','A business owner organizing work on a laptop',[
 ('Notice What Keeps Returning','For a few working days, note the tasks and questions that come back to you. Distinguish decisions that need your experience from repeated work that reaches you because nobody else has clear ownership.','Do not assume that every recurring interruption should be delegated immediately. Some work needs a better process, better information, or a clearer boundary before another person can take responsibility for it.'),
 ('Define What Complete Looks Like','Choose one recurring task. Explain the outcome, the information needed, the expected standard, and how completion is recorded. Show an ordinary example and a situation that should be escalated.','The goal is enough clarity for someone to act without reconstructing your judgment every time. Document the important decisions in language the person doing the work can use. Leave room for questions about exceptions.'),
 ('Agree the Decision Boundaries','Discuss what the person can decide, what requires your approval, and which situations need immediate attention. Agree how and when you will review the work. Different tasks need different levels of visibility.','Check capacity as well as skill. Giving ownership to someone who already has a full workload can simply move the bottleneck. The surrounding schedule and information need to support the responsibility.'),
 ('Review and Refine Together','Begin with a manageable scope. Review actual examples rather than checking every action continuously. Ask what was unclear, which information was missing, and where the work still depended on you.','Use the review to improve the task definition and boundaries. Useful delegation should create dependable ownership while giving the business owner enough visibility to remain accountable.')],
 'Choose one task that repeatedly returns to you. Write its desired outcome, completion standard, decision limits, escalation point, and review rhythm.'),
 ('connect-the-handoff','Operations','Make the Handoff Clear.','Reduce the uncertainty between one person finishing and the next person beginning.','collaboration','Colleagues coordinating work with notes and a laptop',[
 ('Look Between the Tasks','A workflow can have capable people at every stage and still lose time between them. Choose one handoff that frequently needs chasing. Ask both people how they know the work is ready to move and what they expect to receive.','Their answers may differ. One person may think sending a message completes the task. The next may be waiting for a document, a customer detail, or an approval. That gap is worth making visible.'),
 ('Define the Minimum Information','List what the receiving person needs to act. Keep the list connected to the work: customer context, scope, deadlines, decisions already made, and the location of relevant information.','Avoid creating another form that nobody uses. Start with the information that is repeatedly missing. Decide where it should live so people do not need to search across multiple conversations.'),
 ('Make Acceptance and Ownership Explicit','Agree what triggers the handoff and who owns the next step. Include an exception path for incomplete information or unavailable team members. A shared queue does not create ownership by itself.','Discuss the point at which responsibility changes. The sending person should know whether the handoff has been accepted, and the receiving person should know what action is expected next.'),
 ('Check Real Work After the Change','Follow a small number of handoffs after the new approach is in use. Look for waiting, returned tasks, repeated questions, or work without an owner. Ask the team whether the information is sufficient.','Refine the handoff based on those examples. Keep necessary checks, remove avoidable duplication, and make sure the process still works when a different person handles the task.')],
 'For one handoff, agree five things: the trigger, the next owner, the information required, acceptance, and the exception path.'),
 ('before-you-automate','Systems','Before You Automate, Understand the Work.','Choose a workflow worth improving before choosing another tool.','business-systems-900','A computer and connected business tools at a service counter',[
 ('Describe the Current Workflow','Choose a repeated task and explain its trigger, inputs, decisions, exceptions, and output. Observe a real example. The process people describe from memory may skip the checking, copying, and clarification that actually take time.','Separate ordinary steps from judgment calls. Ask where information comes from, who relies on it, and what happens if it is missing or wrong. Those answers help define where automation could assist.'),
 ('Fix Unclear Ownership First','Automation can move information, but it does not resolve an unanswered question about who is responsible. Agree the owner of the workflow and the people who need to review or act on its output.','Consider whether the process needs to change before it is automated. A clearer handoff, a simpler input, or the removal of an unnecessary approval may address part of the problem without adding technology.'),
 ('Plan for Exceptions and Review','Identify what should happen when the usual path fails. Consider incomplete records, duplicate requests, unavailable services, and decisions that need a person. Define how the owner notices a problem and how work resumes.','Decide what information the workflow truly needs and who should have access. A useful design keeps human review at the points where context, consequences, or uncertainty require it.'),
 ('Start With a Bounded Use','Select a manageable workflow and agree what improvement would make it worthwhile. Compare the new process with the starting point, including review time, errors, maintenance, and any extra manual work.','Treat the first version as something to observe and improve. Keep clear ownership for maintaining it as the business changes, and expand only when the experience supports doing so.')],
 'Before choosing a tool, write down the trigger, input, owner, output, exceptions, and one useful measure of improvement.'),
 ('website-next-step','Systems','Does Your Website Make the Next Step Clear?','Review the customer journey from recognition to a useful conversation.','register-counter','A business owner using tools at a customer service counter',[
 ('Enter as a Customer','Start on the page a customer is likely to see first. Ask whether it helps them recognize their situation and understand why the business is relevant. Read it without relying on knowledge that only your team has.','A list of services can be accurate and still leave a visitor unsure where to begin. Consider the question the person is trying to answer at each point, and whether the page answers it in familiar language.'),
 ('Give Each Page a Job','Decide what the visitor should understand after reading the page. A home page may create recognition. Another page may explain the process, establish trust, or make contacting the business easier.','Look at the transition between pages. Does the next link follow naturally from what the visitor just learned? Consistent language and a clear route can help the site feel like one connected experience.'),
 ('Review the Contact Path','Try to contact the business on a phone-sized screen and with a keyboard. Check whether labels are clear, fields are readable, and the form asks only for information needed to begin.','Explain what happens next. Do not ask a visitor to select a technical solution before they have described the problem. Provide another contact route where appropriate and make any limitations of the form clear.'),
 ('Use Real Questions to Improve It','Ask someone unfamiliar with the business to explain what they think you help with and what they would do next. Notice where they hesitate or need an explanation.','Combine those observations with the customer questions your team already hears. Improve the parts that create confusion, then check the same path again. The aim is a clearer journey tied to the business’s actual customers.')],
 'Choose one customer question for each page to answer. Then test whether its main link creates the next useful question.')
]
GUIDE_PHOTOS=[('guide-friction-photo','A mechanic inspecting the moving parts of a vehicle'),('guide-inquiry-photo','A café owner preparing a customer order'),('guide-delegation-photo','A kitchen team preparing food together'),('guide-handoff-photo','A craftsperson marking a precise measurement before the next step'),('guide-automation-photo','A business owner welcoming customers at an open shop'),('guide-website-photo','Customers choosing their next step at a food truck')]
for guide_index,(slug,category,title,description,img,alt,blocks,exercise) in enumerate(GUIDES):
 body='<header class="article-header" id="page-top"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Practical guide · '+category+'</p><h1>'+title+'</h1><p>'+description+'</p></div></header>'
 body+='<div class="container"><figure class="guide-lead-photo">'+image(*GUIDE_PHOTOS[guide_index])+'</figure></div>'
 body+='<div class="container"><article class="article-body"><a class="article-back" href="resources.html">← Back to resources</a>'
 body+='<nav class="guide-contents" aria-label="In this guide"><strong>In this guide</strong>'+''.join(f'<a href="#guide-step-{i}">{i:02} · {t}</a>' for i,(t,a,b) in enumerate(blocks,1))+'</nav>'
 for i,(t,a,b) in enumerate(blocks,1):body+=f'<h2 id="guide-step-{i}">{t}</h2><p>{a}</p><p>{b}</p>'
 body+=f'<aside class="editorial-note"><strong>A useful place to start</strong><p>{exercise}</p></aside><h2>You Can Bring the Situation Before You Know the Solution.</h2><p>Tell us what you noticed, what keeps returning to you, or which part of the work feels harder than it should.</p>{link("lets-talk.html","Talk it through with us",True)}</article></div>'
 write('guide-'+slug+'.html',title,description,body,active='resources.html')

